WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.736
Runtime configuration determines what the
container can do when it runs.

2
00:00:06.816 --> 00:00:13.856
An image may look acceptable in isolation
but become risky when deployed with broad

3
00:00:13.936 --> 00:00:18.663
privileges, sensitive mounts, weak secrets
handling,

4
00:00:18.743 --> 00:00:21.660
or unnecessary network exposure.

5
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:28.981
Reviewers should connect image findings to
the actual runtime configuration rather

6
00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:34.794
than treating container security as a
static image-only exercise.

7
00:00:34.874 --> 00:00:40.909
Running as root is a common concern
because it can increase impact if the

8
00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:45.414
application or container boundary is
compromised.

9
00:00:45.494 --> 00:00:52.836
Linux capabilities control specific
privileged operations available to processes.

10
00:00:52.916 --> 00:00:59.856
Privileged containers receive broad access
to host capabilities and devices and

11
00:00:59.936 --> 00:01:06.775
should be treated as high risk unless
there is a carefully justified operational

12
00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:07.358
need.

13
00:01:07.438 --> 00:01:14.579
The review should ask whether permissions
match the workload's real requirements.

14
00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:21.397
Bind mounts, writable filesystems,
environment variables, resource limits,

15
00:01:21.477 --> 00:01:24.696
and network exposure all affect risk.

16
00:01:24.776 --> 00:01:31.615
A sensitive host path mounted into a
container can create a serious blast-radius

17
00:01:31.695 --> 00:01:32.500
concern.

18
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:39.218
A read-only filesystem can reduce
opportunities for unwanted modification.

19
00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:43.824
Resource limits can help contain runaway
workloads.

20
00:01:43.904 --> 00:01:47.524
Environment variables can be convenient,

21
00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:52.130
but they may expose secrets if not managed
carefully.

22
00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:58.345
AI can help create runtime review
checklists from manifests or approved

23
00:01:58.425 --> 00:02:00.538
configuration exports.

24
00:02:00.618 --> 00:02:07.155
It can organize questions about root
users, capabilities, privileged mode,

25
00:02:07.235 --> 00:02:13.068
mounts, ports, secrets, resource limits,
and filesystem settings.

26
00:02:13.148 --> 00:02:20.490
The reviewer should verify the checklist
against actual deployment context and avoid

27
00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:28.013
recommending changes that would break
required application behavior without a tested

28
00:02:28.093 --> 00:02:29.300
alternative.
